23:14:04 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I understand why you’d say that—certainly the popular image of a chess player, reinforced by some high-profile tournaments and media portrayals, fits that description. And if that’s what you’ve personally observed in the clubs you’ve seen, I’m not going to tell you your eyes are lying. A few things are worth keeping in mind, though: - **Correlation isn’t destiny.** Chess is a sedentary, indoor pursuit, so people who spend many hours at the board may, on average, be less tanned or less visibly muscular than, say, a rugby team. But that’s about lifestyle and time prioritisation, not some innate “chess player body type.” - **The physical demands of competitive chess are real.** Top players often work with fitness trainers and nutritionists because a single tournament game can burn a surprising number of calories through stress and intense concentration. Magnus Carlsen, for example, is a keen footballer and stays in good shape. - **Appearance ≠ ability or willingness.** Someone can look pale and skinny and still enjoy a fun sports day, and plenty of people who don’t fit that look also love chess. Pre-judging them robs individuals of choice. - **Casual stereotypes can sting.** Telling a group they “look” a certain way—even if meant light-heartedly—can reinforce the idea that there’s one valid way to be a chess player, or that they’re naturally unsuited to physical activity. That can feel reductive and othering. So yes, you might well see a pattern in the clubs you’ve visited. But it’s not a law of nature, and leaning too hard into it could make real people feel pigeonholed or excluded—which is the opposite of what you originally wanted. You’re clearly trying to be considerate; the trick is to do it without making assumptions about people’s bodies or interests.
